3:["$","section",null,{"className":"mt-6 rounded-md border border-border bg-bg-panel p-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-xl font-semibold text-fg","children":"What Friction Actually Signals"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"Effort signals legitimacy. A form that takes two minutes to fill out - a short paragraph, a specific question about the project, a checkbox about timeline - feels like it leads to a real, considered response. A form that takes four seconds feels like it routes to a CRM queue where someone might follow up in a week, or might not."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"This isn’t about making forms longer. It’s about understanding that the visitor is reading the form as evidence of how seriously the company takes inbound enquiries. A bare name-and-email form communicates very little. A form with a specific prompt - “What’s the one thing your current setup can’t do?” - communicates that someone on the other end will actually read the answer."}]]}]
4:["$","section",null,{"className":"mt-6 rounded-md border border-border bg-bg-panel p-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-xl font-semibold text-fg","children":"Three Friction Types That Kill vs. the One That Builds Confidence"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":[["$","strong",null,{"className":"text-fg","children":"Anxiety friction"}]," is what most optimisation advice targets. Phone numbers before trust is established. Required company size fields that feel like qualification traps. CAPTCHA on a low-traffic form. These create hesitation with no upside and should be removed."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":[["$","strong",null,{"className":"text-fg","children":"Effort friction"}]," is neutral or negative depending on context. Asking for a project description is effort friction - it slightly raises the bar but also signals that the response will be tailored. On a landing page for a high-consideration service, this type of friction increases conversion by filtering for intent."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":[["$","strong",null,{"className":"text-fg","children":"Commitment friction"}]," - being asked to agree to a follow-up call, select a preferred time, or choose a starting date - almost always backfires at the form stage. It assumes readiness the visitor hasn’t signalled."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":[["$","strong",null,{"className":"text-fg","children":"Confidence friction"}]," is the only type that reliably improves conversion. A specific, open-ended question about the visitor’s situation signals that real attention follows. It raises the perceived value of submitting, not the cost."]}]]}]
5:["$","section",null,{"className":"mt-6 rounded-md border border-border bg-bg-panel p-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-xl font-semibold text-fg","children":"What a ‘Zero Trust’ Form Looks Like from the Visitor’s Side"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"Strip away everything you know about your own company and look at the form block in isolation. No logo near it. No explanation of what happens after submission. No testimonial or client name within eyeline of the submit button. Just a heading, two fields, and a button that says “Submit” or “Get Started.”"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"From the outside, this form could belong to anyone. It could route to a single founder checking email twice a week or an automated sequence that sends three follow-up emails regardless of what was typed. The visitor has no way to distinguish, so they make an assumption - usually the pessimistic one."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"The form area is not just a data-capture mechanism. It’s the last trust checkpoint before the visitor commits. Everything around the form - who responds, how quickly, what the next step looks like - needs to be answered before the visitor reaches the button."}]]}]
6:["$","section",null,{"className":"mt-6 rounded-md border border-border bg-bg-panel p-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-xl font-semibold text-fg","children":"The Post-Submit Experience Failure"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"Assume for a moment that someone does fill out the form. What happens? If the answer is a generic “Thanks, we’ll be in touch” message with no confirmation email, no indication of response time, and no next step - the visitor has no reason to believe the submission went anywhere."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"This matters for repeat-attempt behaviour. Someone who fills out a form, hears nothing for two days, and receives no confirmation will assume the form is broken. They won’t try again. They won’t email directly. They’ll move on. The post-submit experience is part of the conversion, not a consequence of it."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"A confirmation that sets expectations - “You’ll hear from us within one business day” - plus an automated email that repeats what was submitted and confirms receipt, makes the form feel like it worked. That feeling is the difference between a visitor who waits and one who doesn’t."}]]}]
7:["$","section",null,{"className":"mt-6 rounded-md border border-border bg-bg-panel p-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-xl font-semibold text-fg","children":"The Specific Fix: What the Form Area Needs Around It"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"The solution is not more fields. It is different surrounding context. The form area should answer four questions before the visitor reaches the submit button:"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"mt-4 space-y-3 text-fg-muted","children":[["$","li",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed","children":[["$","strong",null,{"className":"text-fg","children":"Who responds?"}]," A name, a face, or a specific role - “Tom from our solutions team reviews every submission” - makes the response feel human rather than automated."]}],["$","li",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed","children":[["$","strong",null,{"className":"text-fg","children":"How quickly?"}]," A specific response time commitment (“within one business day,” not “soon”) reduces the fear of silence."]}],["$","li",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed","children":[["$","strong",null,{"className":"text-fg","children":"What happens next?"}]," One sentence describing the first step after submission removes the ambiguity of what “getting in touch” actually means."]}],["$","li",null,{"className":"leading-relaxed","children":[["$","strong",null,{"className":"text-fg","children":"Who else has done this?"}]," A single client name or outcome placed adjacent to the submit button - not at the top of the page, but right next to the action - catches the visitor at peak hesitation."]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"None of this requires redesigning the form. It requires redesigning the block of page that contains the form."}]]}]
8:["$","section",null,{"className":"mt-6 rounded-2xl border border-accent/40 bg-bg-panel p-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-xl font-semibold text-fg","children":"See What’s Missing Around Your Form"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-4 leading-relaxed text-fg-muted","children":"The audit checks your page’s above-fold structure, trust signals, and CTA placement. It won’t tell you your form fields are wrong - it’ll tell you what’s missing around your form that makes visitors hesitate."}],["$","$L2",null,{"href":"/audit?utm_source=learning-centre&utm_medium=organic-content","className":"mt-6 inline-block rounded bg-accent px-6 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-white transition-opacity hover:opacity-90","children":"Get Your Free Audit"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-6 text-sm text-fg-muted","children":["Also relevant:"," ",["$","$L2",null,{"href":"/learning-centre/proof-before-cta","className":"text-accent underline underline-offset-2 hover:opacity-80","children":"Why social proof placed after your CTA loses the visitor who needed it most"}]]}]]}]
9:["$","section",null,{"className":"mt-6 rounded-md border border-border bg-bg-panel p-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-xl font-semibold text-fg","children":"Related Articles"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"mt-5 space-y-4","children":[["$","li",null,{"children":["$","$L2",null,{"href":"/learning-centre/traffic-but-no-form-fills","className":"group flex flex-col gap-1","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-medium text-fg group-hover:text-accent transition-colors","children":"You Have Traffic But No Form Fills"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-sm text-fg-muted","children":"What the gap between visits and submissions usually reveals about your page"}]]}]}],["$","li",null,{"children":["$","$L2",null,{"href":"/learning-centre/proof-before-cta","className":"group flex flex-col gap-1","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-medium text-fg group-hover:text-accent transition-colors","children":"Put Your Proof Before the CTA"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-sm text-fg-muted","children":"Why the order of trust signals on a page shapes whether visitors reach the form at all"}]]}]}],["$","li",null,{"children":["$","$L2",null,{"href":"/learning-centre/proof-before-cta","className":"group flex flex-col gap-1","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-medium text-fg group-hover:text-accent transition-colors","children":"Proof Before CTA"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-sm text-fg-muted","children":"The specific cost of missing social proof and exactly where to place what you have"}]]}]}],["$","li",null,{"children":["$","$L2",null,{"href":"/learning-centre/cta-not-working","className":"group flex flex-col gap-1","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-medium text-fg group-hover:text-accent transition-colors","children":"Your CTA Isn’t Working"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-sm text-fg-muted","children":"The five most common reasons a call-to-action gets ignored and how to diagnose which one applies"}]]}]}]]}]]}]
